[35m[3mcodex[0m[0m
The correction now preserves the visible behavior and adds two specification-derived boundary checks: runtime-name equality no longer creates false coverage, and `list<int>` can be exhaustive by length even though its elements are unbounded.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
- Closedness is compositional: an unbounded element type does not make list *length* unbounded to the checker.
- `== name` is a possible runtime filter, not a statically known case; treating it as a wildcard was the key false-positive route.
- I retained the compiler-style suppression of an exhaustiveness result when an arm is already malformed, which the supplied `unbound_variable` case establishes.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

