One further consequence of the same scope rule is worth modelling explicitly: `== name` consumes an existing binding, so it must not silently accept a name that no clause head or arm has introduced. I’m adding that boundary check, plus direct clause-head duplicate detection.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
- Binding syntax and matching syntax are deliberately asymmetric: bare `name` introduces; `== name` reads.
- Checking these at the pattern boundary avoids confusing a missing match target with an exhaustiveness problem downstream.
